// ============================================================================
// main.bx — the entry point of the Burxt compiler, written in Burxt.
//
// **This file was called `stage1.bx` until v0.0.215, and the rename is the point.**
// Every one of its neighbours is named for what it DOES — `parser.bx`, `check.bx`,
// `emit.bx` — and then the entry point was named for its position in a bootstrap
// sequence. "Stage 1" answers *which step of building something else is this*, which is
// a question only whoever built it ever asks. A maintainer opening this directory asks
// *where does the program start*, and the answer is spelled `main` in every language
// there is — including in `src/rust-compiler/main.rs`, sitting in the other compiler at
// the same place doing the same job.
//
// It is also no longer TRUE in the way the name implied. A stage is a step toward a
// destination you have not reached yet; Burxt compiles Burxt to a byte-identical
// fixpoint, so this is not on the way to a compiler, it IS one. The name outlived what
// it described — the same rot that `spec/M4-SELF-HOSTING.md` §3b was corrected for in
// this same version, and worth recording twice because it happened twice.
//
// (**"stage-0" and "stage-1" survive as PROSE**, in the specs, where the subject really
// is the bootstrap: which compiler built which, and why two of them must agree. That is
// a defined term doing work in a sentence about history. A filename is not a sentence.)
//
// ---- the two compilers, side by side ---------------------------------------------
//
//   src/burxt-compiler/            src/rust-compiler/
//   main.bx     the entry point    main.rs      the entry point and CLI
//   ast.bx    shapes, lexer      ast.rs + lexer.rs
//   parser.bx   tokens in, arena AST out        parser.rs
//   check.bx    scales, regions, purity, contracts, exhaustiveness    typeck.rs
//   emit.bx     textual LLVM IR + the runtime   codegen.rs (LLVM's C API, via inkwell)
//               — and nothing here yet for     diag.rs · lsp.rs · review.rs ·
//                 the tooling                    schema.rs · json.rs
//
// The last row is the honest asymmetry and the only one: the LANGUAGE is covered on both
// sides, the TOOLING is not. See `spec/ROADMAP-1.0.md` §A0.
//
// The program is this file; the compiler is the modules it uses. It was one file of
// nearly six thousand lines until v0.0.82, because Burxt had no module system — and
// splitting it is the acceptance test for having one. If the compiler cannot be
// split, modules are not done.
//
//   ast.bx    the shapes, and the lexer
//   parser.bx   tokens in, arena AST out
//   check.bx    the rules: scales, regions, purity, contracts, exhaustiveness
//   modules.bx  `use "path";`, resolved before lexing
//   emit.bx     textual LLVM IR, and the runtime emitted with it
//
// Build and run it with:
//
//     burxt build src/burxt-compiler/main.bx -o /tmp/burxt-in-burxt
//     /tmp/burxt-in-burxt <program.bx> [out.ll]
//
// Given a second argument it emits LLVM IR for the program; given one it lexes,
// parses and checks it, and reports what it found.
// ============================================================================

use "ast.bx";
use "lexer.bx";
use "parser.bx";
use "check.bx";
use "modules.bx";
use "emit.bx";
use "diag.bx";
use "schema.bx";
use "lsp.bx";
use "review.bx";
use "layout.bx";

// The shell, for `build` and `run`. Those two have to invoke `llc` and `cc`, and until
// v0.0.219 the Burxt compiler could not: it emitted IR to a file and a human ran the rest
// by hand. That is the difference between an IR emitter and a compiler, and it is why this
// one line was the centre of the parity gate rather than the CLI parsing around it.
//
// `system` was already declared in `lib/os.bx` and already in use, so this needed measuring
// rather than designing — the same lesson `getchar` taught one version earlier. The compiler
// modules do not depend on `lib/`, so the declaration is repeated here rather than imported.
external function system(command: String) -> CInt touches commands;

// Standard input, one byte at a time, for `check -`. `getchar` answers -1 at end of file.
//
// It was already declared in `lib/os.bx` and already in use, which is why v0.0.218's claim that
// this was BLOCKED was wrong. The compiler modules do not depend on `lib/`, so the declaration
// is repeated here rather than imported.
external function getchar() -> CInt touches input;

// Where LLVM lives, asked rather than assumed.
//
// This compiler shells out to `llc` because it emits TEXTUAL IR — stage-0 writes objects through
// inkwell and never runs this path, which is exactly why the bug below was invisible for so long:
// only stage-1 needs `llc`, and only a non-Debian machine disagrees about where it is.
//
// The path was hardcoded to `/usr/lib/llvm-18/bin/llc`, which is Debian's layout. On macOS
// **every** stage-1 compile failed — the first two Darwin runners this project ever used both
// reported it, and the message was `No such file or directory` from `sh`, which is at least
// honest about the cause.
//
// The obvious fix does not work. Homebrew's `llvm@18` is **keg-only**, so nothing called `llc` is
// ever on PATH on macOS, and `llc-18` is Debian's spelling rather than a portable one. What both
// CI workflows already export is `LLVM_SYS_181_PREFIX` — the variable inkwell itself needs — so
// the honest question is "where did whoever built me find LLVM", and the answer is already in the
// environment. The Debian path stays as the default so a Linux user notices nothing.
//
// Declared here rather than imported for the same reason as `system` and `getchar`: the compiler
// modules do not depend on `lib/`, so `lib/os.bx`'s `os_env_or` is not reachable from here.
external function getenv(name: String) -> CPointer touches input;

// `LLVM_SYS_181_PREFIX/bin/llc` when that is set, and Debian's path when it is not.
function llc_command() -> String touches input {
    let found: CPointer = getenv("LLVM_SYS_181_PREFIX");
    if c_is_null(found) {
        return "/usr/lib/llvm-18/bin/llc";
    }
    // Copied at the boundary — after this line the bytes are Burxt's and the pointer is gone.
    let prefix: String = c_string_at(found);
    if len(prefix) == 0 {
        return "/usr/lib/llvm-18/bin/llc";
    }
    return prefix + "/bin/llc";
}

// `byte_as_string(b)` used to be DECLARED HERE, as a table of the 95 printable ASCII bytes plus
// three whitespace ones, and it `exit(2)`-ed on anything else — so `burxt check -` could not read a
// source file containing a single non-ASCII character from a pipe. **It is a BUILTIN now (roadmap
// A13), the same name and the same signature, working for all 256 values**, so the declaration is
// gone and the call site below is unchanged.
//
// Kept as a note rather than deleted silently, because the limitation it named was stated in a
// user-facing message: *"non-ASCII source must come from a file"* was true, is no longer, and a
// reader who remembers it needs to be told once. The refusal was the honest shape while there was
// no way to build a byte; the answer was to give the language one.
//
// It is also how the collision was found, and that is worth recording: reserving the name made
// stage-0 refuse this declaration, but the probe pass that infers `allocates` DISCARDS its errors
// (`let _ = probe.check_program_inner(prog)`), so it aborted early, credited nothing, and the only
// visible symptom was an unrelated escape refusal in `lsp.bx` — a file with nothing wrong with it.


// The recorded problem, in the shape asked for. Nothing when there was none, or when the reporters
// printed it themselves (`shape == 0`).
//
// It reads `problem_start` rather than being told the span, so a caller cannot describe one problem
// while the checker recorded another.
function report_problem(mutable unit: Unit, src: String, path: String, shape: Int) -> Int touches files {
    if shape == 0 || unit.problem_start < 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    // **The span is an offset into the CONCATENATED buffer, not into `path`.** Translating it is
    // the difference between a position and a wrong answer: before v0.0.239 a problem in a program
    // that `use`s a library reported line 1543 of a 13-line file, with a byte-identical message
    // above it. The words looked right, which is why nobody noticed.
    //
    // So the file is looked up and the offset rebased, exactly as stage-0's `locate_file` does — and
    // the SOURCE handed to the renderer is that file's own text, because a caret block has to echo
    // the line the reader can open.
    let named: String = file_at_offset(path, unit.problem_start);
    let start: Int = offset_within_file(path, unit.problem_start);
    let stop: Int = start + unit.problem_length;
    let shown: String = read_file(named);
    if shape == 2 {
        print(diag_json(named, shown, start, stop, unit.problem_message));
        return 1;
    }
    // The caret block ends in a newline of its own and `print_error` adds one, so the last byte is
    // dropped and the stream supplies it — the same trick `tests/support/diag_harness.bx` uses, and
    // for the same reason: trimming in the caller would hide a renderer that forgot the newline.
    let block: String = diag_render(named, shown, start, stop, unit.problem_message);
    print_error(substring(block, 0, len(block) - 1));
    return 1;
}

// ---- the pipeline, once ----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **A `Unit` cannot be RETURNED from a function, and that is not a bug.** The first version of
// this file factored the arena out into a maker function, and the compiler refused it by name:
//
//     function `fresh_unit` cannot return Unit, because its storage lives in a region and
//     would not outlive it. Fill an array the caller owns, or return a scalar summary.
//
// That is the region model's stated cost — `DESIGN.md` calls the granularity "the honest limit
// of this model, accepted deliberately" — arriving as a sentence at the moment it applies,
// which is the entire argument for the language. So the arena is built where it is used, and
// `drive` owns one for the length of one compilation.
//
// It has a consequence worth flagging for `review.bx`, which needs TWO units to compare two
// files: they cannot be handed to it by a factory. Both must be local to whatever function
// does the comparing, or the comparison runs twice and keeps a scalar summary between passes.

// The IR module: the runtime's globals, then everything the emitter built.
//
// The globals are here rather than in `emit.bx` because they are the same for every program
// — a preamble, not a product of the source. Four of them carry a length header because they
// are Burxt Strings that `to_string` can answer with; the `fmt_*` ones are C format strings
// and must not.
function module_text(mutable unit: Unit) -> String {
return "; LLVM IR written by the Burxt compiler, in Burxt.\n"
    + "@burxt.fmt_int = private unnamed_addr constant [5 x i8] c\"%lld\\00\"\n"
    + "@burxt.fmt_str = private unnamed_addr constant [3 x i8] c\"%s\\00\"\n"
    + "@burxt.fmt_nl = private unnamed_addr constant [2 x i8] c\"\\0A\\00\"\n"
    // These four are Burxt STRINGS, not printf formats, so they carry a length
    // header like every other String — `to_string(true)` answers one of them, and
    // `len` of it would otherwise read whatever the linker placed before the bytes.
    // The `fmt_*` globals above are C format strings and need none.
    + "@burxt.string_true = private unnamed_addr constant \{ i64, [5 x i8] \} "
    + "\{ i64 4, [5 x i8] c\"true\\00\" \}\n"
    + "@burxt.string_false = private unnamed_addr constant \{ i64, [6 x i8] \} "
    + "\{ i64 5, [6 x i8] c\"false\\00\" \}\n"
    + "@burxt.string_minus = private unnamed_addr constant \{ i64, [2 x i8] \} "
    + "\{ i64 1, [2 x i8] c\"-\\00\" \}\n"
    + "@burxt.string_empty = private unnamed_addr constant \{ i64, [1 x i8] \} "
    + "\{ i64 0, [1 x i8] c\"\\00\" \}\n"
    // B19. These three said something different from stage-0, and nothing compared them:
    // `the_two_compilers_render_a_problem_identically` covers COMPILE-TIME diagnostics, and
    // runtime text was checked by no test at all until the B18 tightening required the named
    // message. Same blind spot as B18, one layer up — a program that dies correctly can still
    // say the wrong thing about why, and for a language whose case is that errors read as
    // advice, the wording IS the feature.
    + "@burxt.overflow_msg = private unnamed_addr constant [97 x i8] "
    + "c\"burxt runtime error: arithmetic overflow \\E2\\80\\94 the exact result no "
    + "longer fits in the value range\\0A\\00\"\n"
    + "@burxt.region_msg = private unnamed_addr constant [87 x i8] "
    + "c\"burxt runtime error: region memory exhausted — this build "
    + "reserves 4 GB per process\\0A\\00\"\n"
    + "@burxt.divzero_msg = private unnamed_addr constant [39 x i8] "
    + "c\"burxt runtime error: division by zero\\0A\\00\"\n"
    // The third number was the SOURCE BYTE OFFSET, which is a compiler-writer's number: it
    // tells a reader where in the file the indexing expression sits, not what they may
    // legally pass. Stage-0 prints the last valid index instead, which is the number that
    // ends the argument about whether 5 was allowed.
    + "@burxt.bounds_msg = private unnamed_addr constant [109 x i8] "
    + "c\"burxt runtime error: index %lld is out of bounds \\E2\\80\\94 this array "
    + "holds %lld values (valid indexes 0 to %lld)\\0A\\00\"\n"
    // `argument(n)` had NO message of its own — it borrowed the array bounds check, so a
    // program given one argument and asked for its hundredth said "index 99 is outside an
    // array of 1", naming an array the programmer never wrote. Structural, not wording.
    + "@burxt.argument_msg = private unnamed_addr constant [114 x i8] "
    + "c\"burxt runtime error: argument(%lld) does not exist \\E2\\80\\94 this program "
    + "was given %lld arguments (0 is its own name)\\0A\\00\"\n"
    + unit.finish_globals() + "\n" + unit.finish();
}

// Everything a program needs to become IR, in one place: load it with what it uses, lex,
// parse, check, and emit if asked.
//
// `out_ll` empty means check only. `loud` is 1 for the legacy report — the token counts and
// per-phase work that `spec/M9-PERFORMANCE.md` reads, and that three runner invariants parse
// — and 0 for the subcommands, which must be quiet on success the way every other compiler
// is. Both paths share this function so the two cannot drift, which is the whole reason it
// is a function and not two copies.
//
// Answers the number of problems found: 0 means it worked.
// Given a PATH: the program and everything it uses, in one buffer. The same design stage-0
// uses, for the same reason — a Span stays a byte range, and only a renderer needs to know
// which file an offset fell in. See spec/M6-MODULES.md §1.5.
function drive(path: String, out_ll: String, loud: Int) -> Int touches files {
    return drive_source(load_program(path), path, out_ll, loud);
}

// Given the TEXT, which is what `check -` has: there is no file, so there is nothing to
// resolve `use` against.
//
// **That matches the Rust build exactly, and I checked rather than assumed.** `burxt check -`
// in Rust bypasses the module pre-pass entirely — `use "lib/option.bx"` piped into it is a
// SYNTAX ERROR, not a resolved import. So this split is not a shortcut; resolving `use` here
// would make the Burxt compiler MORE capable than the Rust one and therefore divergent, which
// under the parity gate is a defect rather than a feature. If stdin should resolve imports, it
// should start doing so in both compilers in the same version, against a directory someone
// chose on purpose.
//
// It also removes a temp file the first version wrote into the current directory — which
// `the_repository_root_holds_only_what_belongs_there` would have failed on the first time
// anyone ran `check -` from the repository root.
function drive_source(src: String, path: String, out_ll: String, loud: Int) -> Int touches files {
    return drive_reporting(src, path, out_ll, loud, 0);
}

// The same pipeline, with a choice of how a problem is REPORTED.
//
// `shape` is 0 for the plain line the compiler has always printed, 1 for the caret block, 2 for one
// line of JSON. All three come from the same recording — `check.bx` and `parser.bx` remember the
// FIRST problem's span behind a `diagnose` flag and print nothing when it is set — so the three
// shapes cannot disagree about which problem they are describing, which is the failure a second
// rendering path would have.
//
// The renderers are `diag.bx`'s, already held byte-for-byte against `diag.rs` since v0.0.222,
// LSP positions included. So this function chooses; it does not format.
function drive_reporting(src: String, path: String, out_ll: String, loud: Int,
                         shape: Int) -> Int touches files {
    let mutable unit: Unit = Unit { tokens: [], keywords: [], nodes: [], children: [], nested_children: [], items: [], bindings: [], consts: [], functions: [], stack: [], methods: [], types: [], params_in_scope: [], generics: [], type_parameters: [], type_param_bounds: [], pending_bounds: [], current_generic: 0 - 1, current_method: 0 - 1, probing: 0, probe_changes: 0, grow_parameters: [], grow_self: [], relay_parameters: [], relay_self: [], relay_method_parameters: [], type_bindings: [], measure_clause: 0 - 1, measure_slot: 0, measure_parameters: 0 - 1, measure_owner: "", old_nodes: [], old_slots: [], ensures_start: 0, ensures_count: 0, ensures_owner: "", bracket_clauses: [], clause_subjects: [], it_token: 0 - 1, it_is_result: 0, used_it: 0, it_seen: 0, result_type_node: 0 - 1, result_slot: 0 - 1, returns_node: 0 - 1, walked_types: [], type_binding_floor: 0, allowed_effects: 63, effects_owner: "", function_buckets: [], function_chain: [], instances: [], instance_arguments: [], impls: [], typed: [], region_depth: 0, region_token: 0 - 1, in_caller_region: 0, in_pure: 0, loop_depth: 0, in_contract: 0, in_ensures: 0, current_function: -1, chunks: [], pending: "", globals: "", global_chunks: [], sret: "", sret_cells: 0, function_entry: "", body_chunks: [], body_pending: "", next_register: 0, next_slot: 0, next_label: 0, slots: [], strings: [], declared: [], loop_condition_labels: [], loop_end_labels: [], region_mark: "", region_loop_depth: 0, in_function: 0, depth: 0, problems: 0, diagnose: 0, problem_start: 0 - 1, problem_length: 0, problem_message: "", position: 0, token_end: 0, no_record_literal: 0, in_extern_signature: 0, range_bounds: 0, in_multiply: 0, print_to_stderr: 0, int_type_node: 0 - 1, errors: 0, type_of_calls: 0, find_binding_calls: 0, find_function_calls: 0, show_type_calls: 0, expr_allocates_calls: 0, type_at_calls: 0 };
    if shape > 0 {
        unit.diagnose = 1;
    }
    let installed: Int = unit.install_keywords();
    let total: Int = unit.run(src);

    if loud == 1 {
        print("lexed " + path + ": " + to_string(total) + " tokens, "
              + to_string(installed) + " keywords known");
        print("  identifiers: " + to_string(unit.count_of(1)));
        print("  decimals:    " + to_string(unit.count_of(3)));
        print("  strings:     " + to_string(unit.count_of(4)));
        print("  errors:      " + to_string(unit.errors));
        print("first tokens:");
        let shown: Int = unit.report(src, 8);
    }
    if unit.errors > 0 && loud == 0 {
        let told: Int = report_problem(unit, src, path, shape);
        return unit.errors;
    }

    let mutable items: Int = 0;
    while !unit.done() {
        let item: Int = unit.parse_item(src);
        if item < 0 {
            break;
        }
        push(unit.items, item);
        items += 1;
    }
    if loud == 1 {
        print("parsed " + to_string(items) + " items and statements into "
              + to_string(len(unit.nodes)) + " nodes, "
              + to_string(len(unit.children)) + " child slots");
        print("  parse errors: " + to_string(unit.errors));
    }
    if unit.errors > 0 {
        let told: Int = report_problem(unit, src, path, shape);
        return unit.errors;
    }

    let problems: Int = unit.check(src);
    if loud == 1 {
        print("checked " + to_string(len(unit.functions)) + " functions and "
              + to_string(len(unit.types)) + " types");
        print("  type errors: " + to_string(problems));
        // M9: what the checker actually did, per run. A number beside a phase is the only
        // thing that turns "the checker is slow" into a place to look.
        print("  work: " + to_string(len(unit.nodes)) + " nodes, type_of "
              + to_string(unit.type_of_calls) + ", find_binding " + to_string(unit.find_binding_calls)
              + " over " + to_string(len(unit.bindings)) + " bindings, find_function "
              + to_string(unit.find_function_calls) + " over " + to_string(len(unit.functions))
              + " functions");
    }
    if problems > 0 {
        let told: Int = report_problem(unit, src, path, shape);
        return problems;
    }
    if len(out_ll) == 0 {
        return 0;
    }

    let emitted: Int = unit.emit_module(src);
    if unit.problems > 0 {
        if loud == 1 {
            print("  backend refusals: " + to_string(unit.problems));
        } else {
            print_error("burxt: the backend refused " + to_string(unit.problems)
                        + " construct(s) in " + path);
        }
        return unit.problems;
    }
    let text: String = module_text(unit);
    let wrote: Int = write_file(out_ll, text);
    if loud == 1 {
        print("wrote " + out_ll + ", " + to_string(len(text)) + " bytes of IR");
    }
    return 0;
}

// ---- the shape of a command line -------------------------------------------------------

// The name a built program should get when nobody said: `pay.bx` in any directory becomes
// `./pay`, which is what `burxt build` in Rust does.
//
// Written out rather than derived from the path by a rule the caller cannot see, because a
// DERIVED name is a reference no sweep can find — `spec/A7.0-NAMING.md` §9 was written after
// exactly that cost three broken tests during the `stage1.bx` rename. Here the derivation is
// unavoidable (it is the default), so it lives in one named function instead of inline.
function default_output_name(path: String) -> String {
    let mutable start: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(path) {
        if byte_at(path, i) == 47 {
            start = i + 1;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    let mutable stop: Int = len(path);
    let mutable j: Int = start;
    while j < len(path) {
        if byte_at(path, j) == 46 {
            stop = j;
        }
        j += 1;
    }
    return "./" + substring(path, start, stop - start);
}

// Is this word a subcommand, or the path of a program?
//
// **This is the compatibility hinge and it is worth stating plainly.** Three runner
// invariants run `stage1 <file.bx>` with no subcommand and read the legacy report — one of
// them parses `type errors:` out of it. So the dispatch below may only fire on a word it
// RECOGNISES; anything else is a path and behaves exactly as it did before. A dispatcher that
// assumed argument 1 was always a command would have broken all three, and the failure would
// have looked like a checker regression rather than a CLI change.
function is_subcommand(word: String) -> Bool {
    if word == "check" || word == "build" || word == "run" || word == "emit-ir" {
        return true;
    }
    if word == "mcp-schema" || word == "review" || word == "layout" {
        return true;
    }
    if word == "--help" || word == "-h" || word == "help" || word == "--version" || word == "-V" {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}

// Everything here goes to STDERR, and so does every other status line in this file.
//
// That is not a style choice, it is what the Rust build does — `eprintln!` for the version, the
// usage, `compiled X -> Y`, and `<file>: no errors` — and a byte-for-byte comparison is the only
// reason I know it. The first version of `check` printed its answer to stdout and the two
// compilers disagreed on a program with no errors at all, which is the least likely place to
// look for a divergence.
//
// The discipline underneath it is the Unix one and it is worth keeping deliberately: **status to
// stderr, product to stdout.** So `burxt emit-ir x.bx > x.ll` writes IR and not a progress
// report, and a CI log shows what went wrong on the stream built for it.
// **The version is a hardcoded string, and it DRIFTED 29 versions before anything noticed.**
//
// `main.rs` prints `env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION")` and therefore cannot drift — it reads the one place
// the version is defined. Burxt has no equivalent: no build script, no compile-time environment
// read, so the number has to be written here by hand. That is the honest cost of the language not
// having the feature, and it is not going away.
//
// What made it a bug rather than a chore: `Cargo.toml`'s bump asserts it replaced exactly one
// thing, and this one used a plain string replace with no assertion. An agent copied its own
// `main.bx` over this file during a merge, reverting the number to its base — and every bump after
// that searched for a string that no longer existed and silently did nothing. **`burxt --version`
// reported 0.0.230 while the compiler was at 0.0.259.**
//
// My own CLI test asserted `--version` output *contains* "burxt" — the word, not the number — so it
// passed throughout. `the_two_compilers_report_the_same_version` in `tests/runner.rs` now compares
// this string against `Cargo.toml`, which is the only thing that can catch a duplicated fact
// diverging: not more care at the bump, but a test that fails when the two disagree.
function usage() -> Int {
    print_error("burxt 0.0.271 — the Burxt compiler, written in Burxt");
    print_error("usage:");
    print_error("  burxt check   <file.bx>                  parse and typecheck only");
    print_error("                <file.bx> --json         ... as JSON, for editors and CI");
    print_error("  burxt build   <file.bx> [-o <path>]      compile to a native executable");
    print_error("  burxt run     <file.bx>                  compile then run");
    print_error("  burxt emit-ir <file.bx>                  print LLVM IR");
    print_error("  burxt <file.bx> [out.ll]                 the phase report, and IR if asked");
    print_error("");
    print_error("  burxt check   -                          ... reading the program from stdin");
    print_error("  burxt build   <file.bx> --target <triple> an object for another machine");
    print_error("");
    print_error("  burxt mcp-schema <file.bx>               the MCP manifest, from the preconditions");
    print_error("");
    print_error("  burxt layout  <file.bx>                  class sizes, alignments, offsets");
    print_error("  burxt review  <old.bx> <new.bx>          what changed about what it PROMISES");
    print_error("  burxt lsp                                language server over stdio");
    print_error("");
    print_error("Not here yet, and named rather than silently missing:");
    print_error("  explain memory — the allocation inference is stage-0's alone until A12");
    print_error("They exist in the Rust build. spec/ROADMAP-1.0.md THE GATE tracks the rest.");
    return 0;
}

// ---- the command line ------------------------------------------------------------------

let mutable command: String = "";
if argument_count() > 1 {
    command = argument(1);
}

if command == "--version" || command == "-V" {
    print_error("burxt 0.0.271 — the Burxt compiler, written in Burxt");
    exit(0);
}

if command == "--help" || command == "-h" || command == "help" {
    let helped: Int = usage();
    exit(0);
}

if command == "lsp" {
    // A language server over stdio. It takes no file: the client sends the buffer, which is why
    // this branch sits above the "needs a file" guard and `lsp` is not in `is_subcommand`.
    //
    // **The protocol owns stdout completely**, so nothing here prints — no banner, no version, no
    // progress. `lsp_serve` writes framed replies with `write(2)` rather than `print`, which is
    // both exact (no trailing newline) and UNBUFFERED: measured replying in 1 ms with the client
    // still holding stdin open, which is the difference between a server and a batch job. The
    // front end's own diagnostics are captured away from fd 1 for the duration of each check.
    //
    // **It must not be wrapped in a `region` block.** `lsp_serve` opens a region per turn, and an
    // inner region inside an outer one hands out the outer arena without ever resetting it — which
    // compiles, and then leaks a compiler's working set per keystroke. That is a real defect and it
    // is written down rather than worked around: see the note in `spec/ROADMAP-1.0.md`.
    let served: Int = lsp_serve();
    exit(served);
}

if is_subcommand(command) && argument_count() < 3 {
    print_error("burxt " + command + ": needs a file");
    exit(2);
}

if command == "check" {
    // `check -` reads the program from standard input, which is what an editor's
    // check-on-type does: the buffer has not been saved, so there is no file to name.
    //
    // Reachable because `getchar` is, which was the correction of v0.0.218 — this row was
    // called BLOCKED on the reasoning that Burxt has no stdin, and `external function
    // getchar()` had been sitting in `lib/os.bx` the whole time. Writing the bytes to a
    // temporary file and compiling that is the honest shape: `load_program` resolves `use`
    // against a DIRECTORY, so a program read from a pipe has to be given one to resolve
    // against, and the current directory is the only defensible choice.
    if argument(2) == "-" {
        let mutable text: String = "";
        let mutable b: Int = getchar();
        while b != 0 - 1 {
            text = text + byte_as_string(b);
            b = getchar();
        }
        // Named `-`, because that is what the Rust build calls it in every message it prints,
        // and a diagnostic that names the file differently in the two compilers is a diagnostic
        // a tool cannot rely on.
        let piped: Int = drive_reporting(text, "-", "", 0, 1);
        if piped == 0 {
            print_error("-: no errors");
            exit(0);
        }
        exit(1);
    }
    // `--json` anywhere after the file, because that is where the Rust build accepts it.
    let mutable shape: Int = 1;
    let mutable a: Int = 3;
    while a < argument_count() {
        if argument(a) == "--json" {
            shape = 2;
        }
        a += 1;
    }
    let problems: Int = drive_reporting(load_program(argument(2)), argument(2), "", 0, shape);
    if problems == 0 {
        print_error(argument(2) + ": no errors");
        exit(0);
    }
    exit(1);
}

if command == "layout" {
    // Each class's size, alignment, and every field's offset — the answer to "why is this record
    // 24 bytes" without reading the emitter.
    //
    // **Known gap, named rather than hidden:** the Rust build also prints the MONOMORPHISED copies
    // of a generic (`Entry$String$Int`), and this does not. 153 of 160 fixtures agree; the 7 that
    // differ all differ for that one reason. `the_two_compilers_report_the_same_layout` holds the
    // count at 7 so it can only fall.
    let shown: Int = layout(argument(2));
    exit(shown);
}

if command == "review" {
    // What changed about what the program PROMISES — signatures, contracts, effects, privacy —
    // rather than what changed textually.
    //
    // `spec/ROADMAP-1.0.md` §C2 makes this the **mechanical semver rule** for the 1.0
    // compatibility promise, which is why it could not stay Rust-only: Burxt would have been
    // unable to enforce its own compatibility promise without Rust, and that is precisely what
    // the gate forbids.
    //
    // Two files, so two arenas — and a `Unit` cannot be returned from a function, so `review.bx`
    // builds both where it uses them rather than taking them from a maker.
    if argument_count() < 4 {
        print_error("burxt review: needs two files — `burxt review old.bx new.bx`");
        exit(2);
    }
    let verdict: Int = review(argument(2), argument(3));
    exit(verdict);
}

if command == "mcp-schema" {
    // The MCP tool manifest, derived from the preconditions. `schema.bx` owns every byte of it,
    // including the note it writes to stderr when a precondition cannot be expressed as a JSON
    // Schema constraint — so this subcommand and `tests/support/schema_harness.bx` cannot produce
    // different output, which would make the parity test test itself.
    let problems: Int = mcp_schema(argument(2));
    if problems > 0 {
        exit(1);
    }
    exit(0);
}

if command == "emit-ir" {
    // Through a file and then read back, because `print` is the only way out and the IR is
    // one string. An honest cost, and the reason it is acceptable: `build` needs the file on
    // disk anyway, so nothing is written that was not needed.
    let scratch: String = "/tmp/burxt-emit-ir.ll";
    let problems: Int = drive(argument(2), scratch, 0);
    if problems > 0 {
        exit(1);
    }
    print(read_file(scratch));
    exit(0);
}

if command == "build" || command == "run" {
    let source: String = argument(2);
    // **`run`'s binary is an intermediate and goes where intermediates go.** The same reasoning
    // `emit-ir`'s scratch file and stage-0's `.o` already follow: `run` means "and then run it", so
    // the binary is a means rather than the product, and leaving one in the working directory is a
    // stray a user gets no warning about — `.gitignore` hides extensionless root files by design, so
    // only a filesystem-walking test sees them. Seven were caught in one day.
    //
    // `build` is unchanged: leaving a file is what `build` is FOR.
    let mutable output: String = default_output_name(source);
    if command == "run" && argument_count() < 4 {
        // The STEM, not the path. My first attempt appended the whole source path and would have
        // built `/tmp/burxt-run-tests/pass/foo.bx` — directories that do not exist. `default_output_name`
        // already answers `./<stem>`, so the two leading characters come off and the rest is reused
        // rather than the stripping logic being written a second place to drift from the first.
        let named: String = default_output_name(source);
        output = "/tmp/burxt-run-" + substring(named, 2, len(named) - 2);
    }
    if argument_count() > 4 {
        if argument(3) == "-o" {
            output = argument(4);
        }
    }
    let ll: String = output + ".ll";
    let object: String = output + ".o";
    let problems: Int = drive(source, ll, 0);
    if problems > 0 {
        exit(1);
    }
    // llc then cc, the same two steps the Rust build makes — it just makes them through
    // LLVM's C API instead of through a shell. The IR is textual either way, which is what
    // makes the two paths comparable at all.
    // `--target <triple>` says which machine the code is FOR, and it stops after the object for
    // the reason the Rust build states: linking needs that target's libc and linker, so it is
    // left to that target's toolchain. The IR is identical for every target, which is what makes
    // the decimal answers identical too — so here the triple only ever reaches `llc`.
    let mutable triple: String = "";
    let mutable a: Int = 3;
    while a < argument_count() {
        if argument(a) == "--target" && a + 1 < argument_count() {
            triple = argument(a + 1);
        }
        a += 1;
    }
    let mutable machine: String = "";
    if len(triple) > 0 {
        machine = " -mtriple=" + triple;
    }
    let assembled: Int = system(llc_command() + " -relocation-model=pic -filetype=obj"
                                + machine + " -o " + object + " " + ll);
    if assembled != 0 {
        print_error("burxt: llc could not assemble " + ll);
        exit(1);
    }
    if len(triple) > 0 {
        // The `.ll` goes and the `.o` STAYS: the object is the product when cross-compiling, since
        // linking needs the other machine's toolchain. Same reason `emit-ir` keeps nothing.
        let dropped: Int = system("rm -f " + ll);
        print_error("wrote " + object + " for " + triple
                    + " — linking needs that target's libc and linker");
        exit(0);
    }
    let linked: Int = system("cc -o " + output + " " + object);
    if linked != 0 {
        print_error("burxt: cc could not link " + object);
        exit(1);
    }
    // The intermediates go, because the Rust build leaves ONLY the executable and this one was
    // leaving `<name>.ll` and `<name>.o` beside it. Found by `the_repository_root_holds_only_what_belongs_there`
    // going red on `absence`, `absence.ll` and `absence.o` after I ran a comparison from the
    // repository root — my own test command polluting the tree, which is how the difference
    // surfaced at all.
    //
    // It is a real divergence and not tidiness: a build that leaves two files behind changes what
    // `ls` shows, what a `.gitignore` has to know about, and what a second build overwrites. Rust
    // hands the IR to LLVM in memory and never has a file to clean up; this one goes through the
    // disk because it emits text, so cleaning up is the cost of that choice rather than an extra.
    let swept: Int = system("rm -f " + ll + " " + object);
    if command == "build" {
        print_error("compiled " + source + " -> " + output);
        exit(0);
    }
    // `run` hands the program's own exit status back to the shell, because a test suite
    // written in Burxt reports failure that way — `lib/test.bx` ends with `exit(test_end(t))`
    // and it would be pointless if the runner swallowed it.
    let ran: Int = system(output);
    exit(divide_floor(ran, 256));
}

// ---- no subcommand: the legacy report, unchanged ---------------------------------------
//
// Three runner invariants depend on every line of this, including the exact text
// `  type errors: N`. It is not a debug mode left in by accident: it is what
// `spec/M9-PERFORMANCE.md` reads to attribute a slowdown to a phase, and what the
// differential tests parse to compare the two checkers.

let mutable path: String = "examples/tour.bx";
if argument_count() > 1 {
    path = argument(1);
}
let mutable out_ll: String = "";
if argument_count() > 2 {
    out_ll = argument(2);
}
let found: Int = drive(path, out_ll, 1);
